1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,070
In 1982,

2
00:00:01,640 --> 00:00:03,560
a man clearing land for a new road in

3
00:00:03,580 --> 00:00:05,780
central Florida dipped his backhoe into a

4
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:06,950
small, muddy pond.

5
00:00:07,460 --> 00:00:09,440
When the bucket came up, something pale

6
00:00:09,460 --> 00:00:10,790
was sitting in the black mud.

7
00:00:11,220 --> 00:00:11,950
Human skulls.

8
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:13,090
Not one.

9
00:00:13,440 --> 00:00:13,730
Several.

10
00:00:14,120 --> 00:00:15,090
The police were called.

11
00:00:15,380 --> 00:00:17,110
Everyone assumed it was a crime scene.

12
00:00:17,320 --> 00:00:19,760
A forgotten murder, perhaps, or an old

13
00:00:19,780 --> 00:00:21,850
family cemetery nobody had written down.

14
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:24,650
But these bones were not decades old.

15
00:00:24,940 --> 00:00:27,130
They were more than 7 ,000 years old.

16
00:00:27,420 --> 00:00:29,060
And when scientists looked inside the

17
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:31,000
skulls, they found something that should

18
00:00:31,020 --> 00:00:31,850
not exist.

19
00:00:32,300 --> 00:00:33,190
Human brains.

20
00:00:33,720 --> 00:00:36,140
Shrunken and dark, but still there, after

21
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:37,810
7 ,000 years underwater.

22
00:00:38,220 --> 00:00:41,490
In the end, the pond gave up 168 people.

23
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:44,060
This is the story of Windover and of the

24
00:00:44,080 --> 00:00:45,990
people who were laid to rest beneath it.

25
00:00:49,780 --> 00:00:51,660
The pond sat near the town of Titusville

26
00:00:51,680 --> 00:00:54,300
on Florida's Atlantic coast, just across

27
00:00:54,320 --> 00:00:56,060
the water from where rockets now launch

28
00:00:56,080 --> 00:00:56,750
into space.

29
00:00:57,240 --> 00:00:58,960
The land was being cleared for a housing

30
00:00:58,980 --> 00:01:01,320
development called Windover Farms, and the

31
00:01:01,340 --> 00:01:02,250
plan was simple.

32
00:01:02,680 --> 00:01:04,750
Fill in the pond, build a road over it.

33
00:01:04,940 --> 00:01:06,620
The medical examiner took one look at the

34
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:08,710
bones and ruled out a modern crime.

35
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:09,830
These were old.

36
00:01:10,140 --> 00:01:11,670
The only question was how old.

37
00:01:12,260 --> 00:01:14,410
What the developers did next was unusual.

38
00:01:14,820 --> 00:01:16,580
Instead of quietly pouring concrete over

39
00:01:16,600 --> 00:01:18,660
the problem, they stopped work and paid

40
00:01:18,680 --> 00:01:19,930
for scientific dating.

41
00:01:20,240 --> 00:01:22,080
For a moment, the future of the site

42
00:01:22,100 --> 00:01:23,960
depended on a few people deciding that the

43
00:01:23,980 --> 00:01:25,660
past mattered more than a construction

44
00:01:25,680 --> 00:01:26,350
schedule.

45
00:01:26,840 --> 00:01:28,680
Two bones were sent to a laboratory for

46
00:01:28,700 --> 00:01:29,950
radiocarbon testing.

47
00:01:30,420 --> 00:01:33,100
The results came back at about 7 ,200 and

48
00:01:33,120 --> 00:01:34,810
7 ,300 years.

49
00:01:35,460 --> 00:01:36,740
That makes these people older than

50
00:01:36,760 --> 00:01:39,480
writing, older than Stonehenge, older than

51
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:41,380
the Great Pyramid of Egypt by more than 2

52
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:42,190
,000 years.

53
00:01:42,780 --> 00:01:44,400
When the Windover people were laid in this

54
00:01:44,420 --> 00:01:46,960
pond, there were no empires anywhere on

55
00:01:46,980 --> 00:01:47,310
Earth.

56
00:01:47,740 --> 00:01:50,570
No kings, no alphabet, no written word.

57
00:01:50,900 --> 00:01:52,680
On the other side of the world, farming

58
00:01:52,700 --> 00:01:54,380
villages were still slowly spreading

59
00:01:54,400 --> 00:01:55,100
across Europe.

60
00:01:55,120 --> 00:01:57,390
And one more thing was already clear.

61
00:01:57,880 --> 00:01:59,440
These people had not been dumped in the

62
00:01:59,460 --> 00:01:59,850
pond.

63
00:02:00,360 --> 00:02:02,740
They had been placed there, carefully, one

64
00:02:02,760 --> 00:02:03,590
by one.

65
00:02:07,660 --> 00:02:09,000
Archaeologists from Florida State

66
00:02:09,020 --> 00:02:11,280
University took over the site, but they

67
00:02:11,300 --> 00:02:13,240
faced a problem no textbook had an answer

68
00:02:13,260 --> 00:02:13,750
for.

69
00:02:14,080 --> 00:02:15,570
Their cemetery was underwater.

70
00:02:16,160 --> 00:02:17,860
Digging blind through mud and water would

71
00:02:17,880 --> 00:02:19,480
destroy the very thing they wanted to

72
00:02:19,500 --> 00:02:19,910
study.

73
00:02:20,300 --> 00:02:21,580
So they came up with an engineering

74
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:22,230
solution.

75
00:02:22,460 --> 00:02:24,160
The team surrounded the pond with a ring

76
00:02:24,180 --> 00:02:25,730
of 160 wells.

77
00:02:26,080 --> 00:02:28,060
Without them, every shovel of peat would

78
00:02:28,080 --> 00:02:29,600
have filled with water the moment it was

79
00:02:29,620 --> 00:02:30,090
lifted.

80
00:02:30,360 --> 00:02:32,340
With them, the bottom of the pond slowly

81
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:33,550
became a dig site.

82
00:02:34,180 --> 00:02:36,160
Pumps drew the groundwater down, lowering

83
00:02:36,180 --> 00:02:37,900
the water table until the peat at the

84
00:02:37,920 --> 00:02:39,520
bottom of the pond could be reached and

85
00:02:39,540 --> 00:02:40,830
excavated by hand.

86
00:02:41,320 --> 00:02:43,100
Season after season, they worked down

87
00:02:43,120 --> 00:02:44,880
through the peat, recording the exact

88
00:02:44,900 --> 00:02:47,020
position of every bone before anything was

89
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:47,550
lifted.

90
00:02:47,780 --> 00:02:49,400
And everything had to be handled with

91
00:02:49,420 --> 00:02:50,280
extreme care.

92
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:52,620
Material that has spent thousands of years

93
00:02:52,640 --> 00:02:54,980
underwater can crack and crumble quickly

94
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:56,870
once it is exposed to open air.

95
00:02:57,240 --> 00:02:59,560
What emerged was not a jumble of bones, it

96
00:02:59,580 --> 00:03:01,110
was an organized cemetery.

97
00:03:01,560 --> 00:03:03,160
About a hundred of the burials had never

98
00:03:03,180 --> 00:03:03,970
been disturbed.

99
00:03:04,420 --> 00:03:05,860
Their skeletons were still fully

100
00:03:05,880 --> 00:03:08,440
articulated, every bone still lying next

101
00:03:08,460 --> 00:03:09,730
to the one it once joined.

102
00:03:10,320 --> 00:03:11,700
Most of the dead had been laid on their

103
00:03:11,720 --> 00:03:13,800
sides, with their knees drawn up toward

104
00:03:13,820 --> 00:03:15,790
the chest, as if they were asleep.

105
00:03:16,320 --> 00:03:18,110
And many of them had been pinned in place.

106
00:03:18,520 --> 00:03:20,100
Sharpened wooden stakes had been driven

107
00:03:20,120 --> 00:03:21,680
down through their wrappings and into the

108
00:03:21,700 --> 00:03:22,410
peat below.

109
00:03:23,380 --> 00:03:25,060
Why would anyone stake the dead to the

110
00:03:25,080 --> 00:03:25,990
bottom of a pond?

111
00:03:26,420 --> 00:03:27,930
One explanation is practical.

112
00:03:28,340 --> 00:03:30,180
A body underwater can float back to the

113
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:30,690
surface.

114
00:03:31,080 --> 00:03:32,430
The stakes held them down.

115
00:03:32,820 --> 00:03:34,380
Whatever the reason, the people of

116
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:36,480
Windover chose water as the resting place

117
00:03:36,500 --> 00:03:37,270
for their dead.

118
00:03:37,540 --> 00:03:39,280
And that water would keep their secret for

119
00:03:39,300 --> 00:03:40,550
7 ,000 years.

120
00:03:44,680 --> 00:03:46,630
As the skulls were lifted out of the peat,

121
00:03:46,800 --> 00:03:48,660
the excavators realized that something was

122
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:49,690
still inside them.

123
00:03:49,920 --> 00:03:52,340
In 91 of the skulls, the excavators found

124
00:03:52,360 --> 00:03:53,990
preserved human brain tissue.

125
00:03:54,600 --> 00:03:56,800
The brains had shrunk and darkened, but

126
00:03:56,820 --> 00:03:58,500
their shape and folds could still be made

127
00:03:58,520 --> 00:03:58,970
out.

128
00:03:59,300 --> 00:04:00,490
And that should have been impossible.

129
00:04:01,020 --> 00:04:02,500
Brain tissue is usually one of the first

130
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:03,930
parts of the body to decay.

131
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:06,400
So how did a human brain survive for 7

132
00:04:06,420 --> 00:04:07,210
,000 years?

133
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:09,570
The answer lies in the pond itself.

134
00:04:09,940 --> 00:04:11,580
The peat at the bottom was water logged

135
00:04:11,600 --> 00:04:12,890
and starved of oxygen.

136
00:04:13,240 --> 00:04:15,060
So the microbes that normally break a body

137
00:04:15,080 --> 00:04:16,260
down could barely work.

138
00:04:16,280 --> 00:04:18,580
The bodies also seem to have gone into the

139
00:04:18,600 --> 00:04:19,870
water soon after death.

140
00:04:20,240 --> 00:04:22,390
The bones show no marks from scavengers,

141
00:04:22,540 --> 00:04:24,260
so animals never had the chance to reach

142
00:04:24,280 --> 00:04:24,690
them.

143
00:04:25,020 --> 00:04:26,970
There was one more crucial ingredient.

144
00:04:27,460 --> 00:04:29,280
Europe's famous bog bodies kept their

145
00:04:29,300 --> 00:04:31,800
skin, but the acidic water of those bogs

146
00:04:31,820 --> 00:04:33,550
slowly dissolved their bones.

147
00:04:34,160 --> 00:04:36,120
At Windover, the water was close to

148
00:04:36,140 --> 00:04:36,610
neutral.

149
00:04:36,960 --> 00:04:38,720
The skin and flesh were gone, but the

150
00:04:38,740 --> 00:04:40,880
bones survived, and so did what was sealed

151
00:04:40,900 --> 00:04:41,890
inside the skulls.

152
00:04:42,300 --> 00:04:44,560
Then came the discovery that made Windover

153
00:04:44,580 --> 00:04:46,950
famous among scientists around the world.

154
00:04:47,240 --> 00:04:48,090
In 1988,

155
00:04:48,720 --> 00:04:49,660
researchers announced that they had

156
00:04:49,680 --> 00:04:51,460
recovered DNA from one of the Windover

157
00:04:51,480 --> 00:04:53,680
brains, one of the oldest human genetic

158
00:04:53,700 --> 00:04:55,510
samples ever studied at the time.

159
00:04:55,840 --> 00:04:57,580
At the time, this was cutting -edge

160
00:04:57,600 --> 00:04:58,270
science.

161
00:04:58,620 --> 00:05:00,900
Only four years earlier, researchers had

162
00:05:00,920 --> 00:05:02,960
recovered DNA from an extinct animal for

163
00:05:02,980 --> 00:05:04,170
the very first time.

164
00:05:04,700 --> 00:05:06,420
Ancient human DNA was a brand new

165
00:05:06,440 --> 00:05:07,070
frontier.

166
00:05:07,620 --> 00:05:09,760
It was a message written in genes, sent

167
00:05:09,780 --> 00:05:11,400
from people who lived thousands of years

168
00:05:11,420 --> 00:05:13,750
before Columbus, before the pyramids,

169
00:05:14,180 --> 00:05:16,230
before almost everything we call history.

170
00:05:16,600 --> 00:05:18,640
And the DNA confirmed what the burials

171
00:05:18,660 --> 00:05:19,870
already suggested.

172
00:05:20,340 --> 00:05:22,960
These were ancient Native Americans, part

173
00:05:22,980 --> 00:05:25,000
of a long story that stretches back to the

174
00:05:25,020 --> 00:05:26,490
first people in the Americas.

175
00:05:30,600 --> 00:05:32,380
The pond preserved something else that

176
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:34,630
almost never survives from the Stone Age.

177
00:05:35,120 --> 00:05:35,650
Cloth.

178
00:05:36,360 --> 00:05:38,510
37 of the graves contained woven fabric.

179
00:05:38,920 --> 00:05:42,130
86 pieces in all, made from plant fibers.

180
00:05:42,580 --> 00:05:44,140
The dead had been wrapped in it before

181
00:05:44,160 --> 00:05:44,730
burial.

182
00:05:45,040 --> 00:05:47,040
Some were bundled in tight, finely made

183
00:05:47,060 --> 00:05:49,970
weaves, others in looser, more open ones.

184
00:05:50,380 --> 00:05:52,050
Think about what that cloth required.

185
00:05:52,500 --> 00:05:54,030
Plant fibers had to be gathered,

186
00:05:54,200 --> 00:05:56,440
processed, twisted into cord, and then

187
00:05:56,460 --> 00:05:57,510
woven by hand.

188
00:05:57,960 --> 00:05:59,980
A single burial wrap could represent many

189
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:00,950
hours of work.

190
00:06:01,420 --> 00:06:03,560
The weaving was complex, using several

191
00:06:03,580 --> 00:06:04,360
different techniques.

192
00:06:04,380 --> 00:06:06,960
These were skilled craftspeople, and their

193
00:06:06,980 --> 00:06:08,680
textiles are among the oldest of their

194
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:09,830
kind ever found.

195
00:06:10,160 --> 00:06:11,780
And that work was not spent on clothing

196
00:06:11,800 --> 00:06:12,510
for the living.

197
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:14,030
It was spent on the dead.

198
00:06:14,420 --> 00:06:15,860
That tells us something about how much

199
00:06:15,880 --> 00:06:17,530
these people mattered to each other.

200
00:06:17,960 --> 00:06:19,820
Alongside the bodies lay the objects of

201
00:06:19,840 --> 00:06:20,690
everyday life.

202
00:06:21,220 --> 00:06:22,450
Tools of bone and antler.

203
00:06:22,980 --> 00:06:23,490
Shark teeth.

204
00:06:23,940 --> 00:06:25,720
Pieces of bottle gourd, used as

205
00:06:25,740 --> 00:06:26,290
containers.

206
00:06:26,980 --> 00:06:29,070
These people had no metal and no pottery.

207
00:06:29,500 --> 00:06:31,330
Everything they made came from the land,

208
00:06:31,560 --> 00:06:33,200
the water and the animals around them.

209
00:06:33,220 --> 00:06:35,620
They lived from Florida's rivers, marshes

210
00:06:35,640 --> 00:06:37,780
and coast, moving with the seasons and

211
00:06:37,800 --> 00:06:40,560
returning, again and again, to this one

212
00:06:40,580 --> 00:06:41,450
small pond.

213
00:06:41,760 --> 00:06:43,930
The care in these burials is unmistakable.

214
00:06:44,220 --> 00:06:46,020
The wrapping, the objects, the careful

215
00:06:46,040 --> 00:06:46,570
placement.

216
00:06:47,100 --> 00:06:48,940
This was a community that honored its

217
00:06:48,960 --> 00:06:49,390
dead.

218
00:06:53,480 --> 00:06:55,570
The bones also tell personal stories.

219
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:57,880
The people buried here ranged from newborn

220
00:06:57,900 --> 00:07:00,370
babies to elders more than 60 years old.

221
00:07:00,780 --> 00:07:02,600
Children were buried here too, many of

222
00:07:02,620 --> 00:07:02,950
them.

223
00:07:03,300 --> 00:07:05,380
In a world without medicine, childhood was

224
00:07:05,400 --> 00:07:05,820
dangerous.

225
00:07:05,840 --> 00:07:08,260
And the pond holds that loss alongside

226
00:07:08,280 --> 00:07:09,330
everything else.

227
00:07:09,800 --> 00:07:11,560
Many of the adults had worn joints and

228
00:07:11,580 --> 00:07:12,170
arthritis.

229
00:07:12,580 --> 00:07:14,500
The marks of hard, physical lives spent

230
00:07:14,520 --> 00:07:16,270
walking, carrying and working.

231
00:07:16,680 --> 00:07:18,440
But one skeleton stopped the researchers

232
00:07:18,460 --> 00:07:19,410
in their tracks.

233
00:07:19,820 --> 00:07:22,190
A boy, about 15 years old.

234
00:07:22,480 --> 00:07:24,760
He had been born with spina bifida, a

235
00:07:24,780 --> 00:07:26,660
condition in which the spine does not form

236
00:07:26,680 --> 00:07:27,270
properly.

237
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:29,700
He had also lost one of his feet, and the

238
00:07:29,720 --> 00:07:31,550
bone of his lower leg had healed over.

239
00:07:31,920 --> 00:07:33,520
He had survived that injury for a long

240
00:07:33,540 --> 00:07:33,900
time.

241
00:07:33,920 --> 00:07:36,200
In a band of hunter -gatherers that moved

242
00:07:36,220 --> 00:07:38,240
through the landscape on foot, a child

243
00:07:38,260 --> 00:07:39,720
like this could not have kept up on his

244
00:07:39,740 --> 00:07:40,150
own.

245
00:07:40,500 --> 00:07:41,840
He would have needed help every single

246
00:07:41,860 --> 00:07:42,330
day.

247
00:07:42,760 --> 00:07:43,610
And he got it.

248
00:07:43,940 --> 00:07:44,850
Someone fed him.

249
00:07:45,160 --> 00:07:46,170
Someone carried him.

250
00:07:46,400 --> 00:07:48,890
Someone cared for him, year after year,

251
00:07:49,040 --> 00:07:50,270
for his entire life.

252
00:07:50,680 --> 00:07:52,330
Think about what that meant in practice.

253
00:07:52,760 --> 00:07:54,940
Every time the group moved, someone had to

254
00:07:54,960 --> 00:07:55,610
carry him.

255
00:07:55,860 --> 00:07:58,110
Every meal, someone made sure he ate.

256
00:07:58,560 --> 00:08:01,140
Not for a week or a season, but for 15

257
00:08:01,160 --> 00:08:01,730
years.

258
00:08:02,140 --> 00:08:05,140
7 ,000 years ago, with no medicine, no

259
00:08:05,160 --> 00:08:07,220
metal, and no writing, the people of

260
00:08:07,240 --> 00:08:08,600
Windover looked after their most

261
00:08:08,620 --> 00:08:09,210
vulnerable.

262
00:08:09,740 --> 00:08:11,620
It is a powerful reminder that compassion

263
00:08:11,640 --> 00:08:13,770
is far older than civilization.

264
00:08:18,040 --> 00:08:19,700
Which brings us back to the strangest

265
00:08:19,720 --> 00:08:20,610
question of all.

266
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:22,450
Why bury the dead in water?

267
00:08:22,860 --> 00:08:24,090
No one knows for certain.

268
00:08:24,320 --> 00:08:25,980
The people of Windover left no written

269
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:28,210
record, only the choices they made,

270
00:08:28,380 --> 00:08:29,470
preserved in the peat.

271
00:08:29,860 --> 00:08:31,680
Burying the dead underwater is rare in the

272
00:08:31,700 --> 00:08:32,620
archaeological record.

273
00:08:32,640 --> 00:08:35,180
Most ancient people buried their dead in

274
00:08:35,200 --> 00:08:37,550
the ground, in caves or in mounds.

275
00:08:38,220 --> 00:08:40,280
Windover is different, and that difference

276
00:08:40,300 --> 00:08:41,470
is part of its mystery.

277
00:08:42,100 --> 00:08:44,370
We do know the pond was not used once.

278
00:08:44,780 --> 00:08:46,890
It was returned to again and again,

279
00:08:47,240 --> 00:08:49,640
generation after generation, as if it were

280
00:08:49,660 --> 00:08:51,390
a place that mattered deeply to them.

281
00:08:51,660 --> 00:08:54,160
Some see the water as a doorway, a passage

282
00:08:54,180 --> 00:08:55,880
between the world of the living and the

283
00:08:55,900 --> 00:08:56,790
world of the dead.

284
00:08:57,520 --> 00:08:58,890
Others think the reason was practical.

285
00:08:59,440 --> 00:09:01,160
Under the water, the dead were safe from

286
00:09:01,180 --> 00:09:03,200
animals, and safe from the heat of the

287
00:09:03,220 --> 00:09:04,010
Florida sun.

288
00:09:04,560 --> 00:09:05,530
Perhaps it was both.

289
00:09:05,960 --> 00:09:07,920
A sacred place that also protected the

290
00:09:07,940 --> 00:09:08,850
people they loved.

291
00:09:09,160 --> 00:09:10,960
Whatever they believed, their choice

292
00:09:10,980 --> 00:09:12,340
worked better than they could ever have

293
00:09:12,360 --> 00:09:12,950
imagined.

294
00:09:17,100 --> 00:09:18,070
In 1987,

295
00:09:18,700 --> 00:09:20,500
Windover was declared a national historic

296
00:09:20,520 --> 00:09:21,150
landmark.

297
00:09:21,620 --> 00:09:23,040
One of the most important archaeological

298
00:09:23,060 --> 00:09:24,510
sites in North America.

299
00:09:24,980 --> 00:09:26,860
Today, the people of Windover are also

300
00:09:26,880 --> 00:09:28,290
part of a larger conversation.

301
00:09:28,780 --> 00:09:30,400
These were ancestors of Native American

302
00:09:30,420 --> 00:09:32,640
communities, and many people believe their

303
00:09:32,660 --> 00:09:34,640
remains deserve not just study, but

304
00:09:34,660 --> 00:09:35,250
respect.

305
00:09:35,700 --> 00:09:37,720
How to balance scientific discovery with

306
00:09:37,740 --> 00:09:39,700
the wishes of indigenous people is a

307
00:09:39,720 --> 00:09:41,500
question archaeology is still learning to

308
00:09:41,520 --> 00:09:42,050
answer.

309
00:09:42,360 --> 00:09:44,190
Windover is part of that story too.

310
00:09:44,700 --> 00:09:46,220
And in a decision that still surprises

311
00:09:46,240 --> 00:09:48,780
people, the archaeologists did not empty

312
00:09:48,800 --> 00:09:49,790
the entire pond.

313
00:09:50,280 --> 00:09:51,580
Part of it was deliberately left

314
00:09:51,600 --> 00:09:54,500
untouched, sealed in its peat, waiting for

315
00:09:54,520 --> 00:09:56,240
scientists with better tools and better

316
00:09:56,260 --> 00:09:57,390
questions than ours.

317
00:09:57,900 --> 00:10:00,060
Somewhere beneath that dark water, more of

318
00:10:00,080 --> 00:10:01,940
the people of Windover may still be lying

319
00:10:01,960 --> 00:10:03,480
exactly where they were placed.

320
00:10:03,500 --> 00:10:06,210
Wrapped in cloth, pinned to the bottom,

321
00:10:06,680 --> 00:10:07,050
waiting.

322
00:10:07,880 --> 00:10:11,840
168 people, 7000 years, and brains that

323
00:10:11,860 --> 00:10:13,050
refuse to disappear.

324
00:10:13,540 --> 00:10:15,710
The pond kept them safe for all that time.

325
00:10:16,180 --> 00:10:18,010
Now it is our turn to remember them.

